WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.628
Hey, everyone, this is
 Phil Lord, I'm here with Byron.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:04.754
My name is Byron Hamel, 

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:06.756
I am a 2021 Academy

00:00:06.756 --> 00:00:08.425
Nicholl fellow and my script, 

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:09.634
Shade of the Grapefruit Tree, 

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:13.513
is about a severely abused white
 boy with a murderous stepdad

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:16.516
and then his black landlady,
 who is obsessed with

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:17.350
science fiction. 

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.562
And she teaches him to behave
 like a robot which empowers him

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:22.731
to confront the bad stepdad.

00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:27.610
Oh, there he is. What's happening? 

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.653
What's up, man?

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:30.030
Hey, really nice to meet you.

00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:31.197
Nice to meet you, too. 

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:34.743
I read your bio and you've taken
 a pretty circuitous route

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:37.078
to professional screenwriting.

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:38.663
It was like I did all these 

00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:42.834
other things to eventually make
 movies, things like journalism.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:44.878
I was an associate
 producer for radio.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:46.212
I was in a band. 

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:48.465
I was a professional actor
 for a little bit. 

00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:49.549
It all applies.

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.009
I love that contrast. 

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.343
You grow up
 in the projects, 

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.095
but then you go to
 a fancy art school. 

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:55.055
This has been my life. 

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:58.016
Like, I love beauty
 and like hardship.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:00:59.225
Just boom, boom, boom. 

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.853
You had all these details
 that were just seemed like

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:03.855
they were things
 that you experienced yourself. 

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.732
Well, these were
 real people know 

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:08.318
I based it on the real people
 and real events in my life.

00:01:08.318 --> 00:01:09.110
I mean, I didn't 

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.155
literally become a robot
 in my real life.

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:12.864
So far.

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:14.324
Yeah, so far I'm working on it.

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:18.328
How long ago did
 you start writing that script?

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.164
It was this time
 last year, actually.

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:22.540
Do you write fast? 

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.460
It really varies
 like it's it's months at a time.

00:01:25.502 --> 00:01:26.586
My first drafts that 

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:29.089
come out, I want it to be
 very pleasing to me.

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:31.091
Does it make my chest go

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:32.008
”Arrgh”

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:32.842
Does it seize me up?

00:01:32.842 --> 00:01:33.134
 Yeah.

00:01:33.134 --> 00:01:35.220
Am I going to cry? The writer?

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:35.970
Is that a big part 

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.973
of your writing process
 that you revise heavily?

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:42.435
I mean, my first draft
 is always garbage.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.353
Same here.

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:46.648
I'm a great editor, actually.
 And that's where I shine.

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:50.026
So if I make garbage,
 it's OK. I can change it.

00:01:50.568 --> 00:01:52.904
I don't have the bone anymore

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:56.032
that that says, “Oh,
 I feel hurt by your comment.”

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:57.700
You have to have
 a lot of humility 

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.953
because the movie
 wants what it wants.

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:04.124
I write with a partner
 all the time, and I learned

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:08.670
not to argue with him
 about his response to it

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.049
and only to try to engage on
 like the problem solving.

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:16.302
And that gets really fun
 because when you've got

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.471
two people in a room
 kicking ideas around, 

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:19.389
you always come up with 

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:22.267
something that neither of you
 could have done on your own.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.394
Yup, because you create
 a whole new entity. 

00:02:24.394 --> 00:02:27.105
It's not you,
 it's not him or her.

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:28.898
It's like the two
 of you together. 

00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:30.024
You're something else. 

00:02:30.024 --> 00:02:32.735
My girlfriend, who really is my
 creative partner in everything.

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:34.028
It is that partnership 

00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:36.447
that makes me find
 the magic moments

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.740
because I didn't
 want to write drama. 

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.119
I wanted to do genre stuff
 because I have all these scenes

00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:44.581
that would be horribly traumatic
 to put a child actor through.

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:48.334
I don't want to be a person
 who some child remembers

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.628
and hates, you know,
 because I put them through that.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:52.422
Yeah. Like, I've often been to 

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:54.424
movies where I'm like,
 I'm being punished, 

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:55.717
and I thought that you found 

00:02:55.717 --> 00:02:58.511
a way without pulling
 any punches to show

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.555
how difficult the situation was. 

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:02.348
But alongside it, 

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:03.892
you know, this is
 what evil looks like, 

00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:05.810
but this is also what
goodness  looks like.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:06.477
Mm-Hmm.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:09.772
The script really
 is about the tiny, small things

00:03:09.772 --> 00:03:12.984
that a person can do to help
 someone who's going through it.

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:15.445
Somebody told me
 when I moved to L.A.

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:19.282
follow the green lights, and
 I always try to remember that

00:03:19.324 --> 00:03:23.077
that when something comes into
 your eyeline, pay attention.

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:25.955
You know, opportunities
 like this, I get to talk to you,

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:29.375
Are selfishly really
 enlightening for me.

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.628
So I got a lot out of that.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:34.005
It's really inspiring. 

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:37.550
Reading your, this work,
it opens up our writing

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.719
me and Chris, when we
 start to think, OK, 

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.055
what if everybody in this scene
 had good intentions?

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:45.725
You've been invited
 into somebody's brain, 

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:46.809
so you want to leave- 

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:49.020
You want to be proud
 of what you're leaving there. 

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:52.357
You know, now you're going to be
 in the position where people

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:55.360
are going to ask you, like,
 what helped your writing

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.694
turn the corner? 

00:03:56.694 --> 00:04:00.031
100 percent write
completely from your heart.

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:03.618
Then worry about how it fits
 with everyone else later.

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:08.373
Something I try to do
 more often is embrace whatever

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.250
enters your head, 

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.710
because it's probably
 you're the only guy who's going

00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:14.045
that's going to occur to.

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:17.340
It’s the only way to tell a story
 that no one else can tell.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.634
And that is how I found my voice
 with this script.

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:21.010
It's funny that it's 

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.805
the thing that broke through for
 you is the most personal.

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:25.348
It is.

00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:26.140
Well done. 

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.518
Well, we'll see you around
 the campus, I'm sure.

00:04:28.518 --> 00:04:31.396
And godspeed.
Do some more, please.

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:32.522
I will. I am.

